EXT. FROZEN LAKE - WHITEOUT - DAY

Wind. Nothing but wind and white. A lone figure kneels on a sheet of gray ice around a hole cut with a hand auger.

ASHER VANTERPOOL (30s, parka crusted with frost, one glove missing) hauls a line. He wedges his heels against the ice. A walkie-talkie sits by his knee, crackling.

RANGER (V.O.)
(broken, windy)
...lake team...do you...ice thinning...south shore...

Asher grabs the walkie. Does not answer. Lets it fall back onto the ice.

RANGER (V.O.) (CONT'D)
...move...north...now...

The line goes taut. Something enormous tugs. He hauls. Ice groans. A hairline crack races outward from the hole.

He keeps pulling.

ASHER
(under his breath)
Come on.

RANGER (V.O.)
...Asher...do you copy...

ASHER ^
Not yet.

The crack widens. His boots slide. He braces. Pulls.

The fish breaks the surface: a gray, glistening length of lake trout, fat as a man's thigh. It flops on the ice.

He stares at it. Takes his bare hand and presses it to the cold flank, once, gently. Then he cuts the line, lifts the trout against his chest like something fragile, and stands.

The ice groans again, deeper. He picks up the walkie.

ASHER (CONT'D)
(into radio)
Heading north.

He walks.
